      "sequence_position": 1,
      "problem": "Given that $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t),\\,{t}^{2}+4t⟩,$ what is ${r}^{′}(t)?$\n\n${r}^{′}(t)=⟨${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$⟩$",
      "worked_solution": "We differentiate each component separately.\n\n- For the $x$-component $x(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t),$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\n- For the $y$-component $y(t)={t}^{2}+4t,$ we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(t)=2t+4.\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨{x}^{′}(t),\\,{y}^{′}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),\\,2t+4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
